* R131 sec 11.8 PROBE -- DOES A SAVED ROW'S DBF_ROOT DESCRIBE ITS CONTENTS?
*
* NOT A SPEC. No markers, no promotion, asserts nothing. It exists to turn a
* source reading into a measurement before an owner rules on 11.8, because the
* first source reading was WRONG and was withdrawn (see below).
*
* SELF-ISOLATING, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON IT CAN BE RUN AT ALL. The
* WORKSPACES slot is redirected to a scratch directory, so every row this
* writes lands in a THROWAWAY catalog and the production catalog is never
* opened. `SET PATH WORKSPACES <dir>` is a real form -- demonstrated live
* 2026-08-30 by the owner. A prior claim in this lane that SET PATH could not
* reach the WORKSPACES slot was wrong: it came from grepping Slot:: in
* cmd_setpath_command.cpp, whose own line 75 says the forms route through
* SETPATH.
*
* WHAT WAS WITHDRAWN. The first reading of 11.8 claimed that
* `SET PATH DBF X IN WSA` followed by a save would record the SESSION's root
* rather than WSA's stamp. It does not: SAVE saves the CURRENT workspace tree,
* and after R131 phase 9 the current workspace's stamp and the session slots
* are held EQUAL -- the bare SET PATH writes both, and SWITCH writes the global
* FROM the stamp. For the ordinary save the column is right, by invariant
* rather than by design.
*
* WHAT IS ACTUALLY IN QUESTION. SaveScope (cmd_workspace.cpp:2125) can span
* MORE THAN ONE ENVIRONMENT two ways: `SAVE <name> ALL` sweeps every open area
* INCLUDING areas owned by other workspaces, and the scoped walk descends into
* CHILDREN, which may carry their own stamps. Both catalog write sites record
* s8(dbf_root()) -- the SESSION slot, which is the SAVER's environment. So the
* question this probe asks is narrow and answerable:
*
*   WHEN A MEMO SPANS TWO ROOTS, WHAT DOES ITS ONE DBF_ROOT COLUMN SAY?
*
* Expected if the column records the saver: DBF_ROOT reads R131B while the
* memo also carries an area opened under R131A. That is not a defect on its
* face -- the column may legitimately mean "where the saver stood" -- but the
* notes at cmd_workspace.cpp:3983 call it "where the workspace CAME from" and
* WRITEBACK uses it as a DEFAULT DESTINATION, and a destination may not be
* guessed. That is the AIF-118 distinction R131 sec 11.6 already ruled with.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* A FRESH SCRATCH CATALOG PER RUN, because the row reads below are positional.
* Run 1 left four rows in tmp/ws118; appending to them would make GO 1..4 read
* run 1's rows and report a stale answer as a fresh one. Run 1's catalog is
* left where it is -- it is the evidence for the truncation finding.
SET PATH WORKSPACES tmp/ws118c
* FIXTURES. Two tables, two roots, different content, so a row that names one
* root can be checked against a memo that provably contains the other.
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131A
ERASE DURA CONFIRM
CREATE X64 DURA (ID N(4), MARK C(8))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "AAA"
CLOSE
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131B
ERASE DURB CONFIRM
CREATE X64 DURB (ID N(4), MARK C(8))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "BBB"
CLOSE
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
* TWO WORKSPACES, TWO DIFFERENT STAMPS, one table open in each.
* NO CLOSE BEFORE THESE TWO USEs, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE -- run 1 of this
* probe (2026-08-30) had one, carried over mechanically from WSENV's blind-arm
* remedy, and IT CLOSED DURWSA'S TABLE ON THE WAY INTO DURWSB. SAVE ALL then
* reported "1 open area(s), every workspace" and the two-environment condition
* the probe exists to create was never built. The WSENV rule is about a USE
* that must be a REAL OPEN in a test arm; here each USE lands in a fresh area
* of a different workspace, and closing first destroys the other workspace's
* area. A remedy is not a reflex.
WORKSPACE NEW DURWSA
WORKSPACE SWITCH DURWSA
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131A
USE DURA
WORKSPACE NEW DURWSB
WORKSPACE SWITCH DURWSB
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131B
* EXPLICIT AREA, AND RUN 2 IS WHY. A bare `USE DURB` here landed on ENGINE
* SLOT 0 -- the slot DURA already occupied under DURWSA -- and evicted it.
* WORKSPACE REGISTRY then read `handle 2 name DURWSA ... members 0`, and
* SAVE ALL reported "1 open area(s), every workspace". Two workspaces, one
* area, and nothing to span. Naming the area is what makes the two
* environments coexist long enough to be saved together.
*
* WHETHER THAT EVICTION IS ITSELF A DEFECT IS NOT CLAIMED HERE. AIF-078 gave
* the allocator find_free_area_for_workspace, and a second workspace's open
* landing on an occupied slot owned by a FIRST workspace is at least worth
* asking about -- but a bare USE is also documented to open in the CURRENT
* area, and DURWSB's local 0 may simply map to engine slot 0. One reading is
* an allocator defect and the other is USE behaving as specified. This probe
* does not distinguish them and must not be cited as though it did.
USE DURB IN 1
* READ THE BASELINE FIRST, AND STOP IF IT IS WRONG. Standing in B, the scoped
* save MUST report "1 area(s)" AND "NOT saved: 1 open area(s) in other
* workspaces". Run 1 printed no NOT-saved line at all, which was the setup
* failing in plain sight above an output nobody checked. If that line is
* missing again, the rest of this probe measures nothing.
WORKSPACE REGISTRY
WORKSPACE SAVE durscoped MEMO
* NOW THE QUESTION. Still standing in B, take everything. The memo will carry
* A's area, opened under R131A, while the saver stands under R131B.
WORKSPACE SAVE durall ALL MEMO
* READ THE CATALOG WITH THE ENGINE, which is the only correct reader for a v64
* table -- a generic DBF reader was pointed at this file on 2026-08-30 and
* returned garbage field descriptors.
* SMARTLIST TRUNCATES AND IT COST RUN 1 ITS ANSWER. Five columns of a 180-wide
* field rendered as ~31 characters, so ...\data\DBF and ...\data\DBF\SANDBOX\
* R131A printed IDENTICALLY. The tell was the next column: IDX_ROOT read
* "...\data\IND", cut mid-word through INDEXES. A field-value read has to be
* WIDER THAN THE FIELD or it is not a read. `?` renders a character field
* whole, so each row is dumped on its own.
SET PATH DBF tmp/ws118c
USE WORKSPACES
GO 1
? "row1_name:" + ALLTRIM(WS_NAME)
? "row1_dbf_root:" + ALLTRIM(DBF_ROOT)
GO 2
? "row2_name:" + ALLTRIM(WS_NAME)
? "row2_dbf_root:" + ALLTRIM(DBF_ROOT)
GO 3
? "row3_name:" + ALLTRIM(WS_NAME)
? "row3_dbf_root:" + ALLTRIM(DBF_ROOT)
GO 4
? "row4_name:" + ALLTRIM(WS_NAME)
? "row4_dbf_root:" + ALLTRIM(DBF_ROOT)
CLOSE
* CLEANUP. The scratch catalog is left in place on purpose: it is the evidence.
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE DESTROY DURWSA
WORKSPACE DESTROY DURWSB
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131A
ERASE DURA CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131B
ERASE DURB CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF
